`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`
Two design choices worth flagging: first, extending `domain.SnapshotStore.Load()` to return `(mrs, writtenAt, error)` rather than bolting on a separate `WrittenAt()` getter method — a stateless interface (call once, get everything) is easier to mock and reason about than one where callers must sequence two method calls correctly. This was only safe because ticket `.1` had added the port with zero real callers, so there was nothing downstream to break.

Second, the `time.Now()` vs `time.Date(...)` gotcha in the round-trip test: `time.Time` carries an internal monotonic-clock reading whenever it comes from `time.Now()`, but `encoding/json` serializes it as an RFC3339 string and `time.Parse` on the way back never reconstructs that reading. Two `time.Time` values representing the identical instant can therefore fail `reflect.DeepEqual` (and thus `assert.Equal`) purely because one has a monotonic component and the other doesn't — building test fixtures with `time.Date(...).UTC()` sidesteps this entirely.
`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`

Bead `mrr-incremental-fetch-3sl.2` is closed. Added `internal/adapters/snapshotstore` (JSON-backed, modeled on `statestore`), extended `domain.SnapshotStore.Load()` to also return the written-at time, wired it into `internal/core`, and covered all four acceptance-criteria cases with tests. `just check` passes clean. Next up in the epic: `.3`/`.4` (the phase-1/phase-2 GitLab fetch) will be the first real consumers of this store.
